\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why verifiable rewards change the experiment}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{post-training}
\tag{rlvr}

\p{RLVR changes the economics and semantics of feedback. A rule or sandbox can
produce many labels without a human judging every trace, and it can make the
success criterion sharper than a learned scalar preference model. But
verifiability is local to the evidence checked. A unit test can be incomplete,
an answer parser can be brittle, a sandbox can leak shortcuts, and a terminal
reward can leave long-horizon credit unresolved. Reward is therefore a
measurement channel, not task truth itself.}

\p{The working protocol definition reconciles several literatures. Classical
RL supplies policies, returns, credit, and off-policy correction;
\citef{shao2024deepseekmath} and \citef{guo2025deepseek} supply group-relative outcome training;
\citef{yu2025dapo} exposes entropy, clipping, dynamic sampling, and length
effects; \citef{miao2026when} studies when costly rollouts can be reused; and
\citef{chen2026lowrank} asks when part of a learned
parameter trajectory can be extrapolated. The last two alter compute use, but
do not by themselves prove capability acquisition.}

\p{The immediate questions are consequently conditional. How does verifier
precision change sample complexity? When does group normalization suppress
signal? Which entropy observable predicts useful exploration rather than
collapse? How much rollout reuse is safe at a given policy divergence? When
does a predictable parameter path merely compress elicitation, and when must a
new environment event supply information that no history-only extrapolator can
recover?}

\p{These questions suggest both positive theorems and counterexamples: bounds
under explicit signal and coverage assumptions, alongside impossibility
results showing that reward, entropy, gradient energy, or low-rank trajectory
geometry cannot serve as universal scalar certificates.}
